A||||19||mshill||Failed CA Emissions test...||||
Z||000000||mshill||09-07-1999||07:36 PM||||OK, I got my cooling system fixed up and burped.  Went to the Smog test station again and failed... (I will be contacting the previous owner).<P>Here's the results:<P>MPH	RPM	HC(ppm)		CO(%)<BR>		MAX	MEAS	MAX	MEAS<BR>15	1582	163	172*	0.85	1.18*<BR>25	2649	113	 86	0.65	0.86*<P>The * indicates failed tests.  My NO emmisions were OK but still relatively high.<P>So I have high hydrocarbon and corbinmonoxide emissions.  Any thoughts?<P>The EGR was replaced a couplke of years ago.<BR>The car is an 86 SE with 188K miles on it.<BR>I am leaning towards the catalytic convertor.<P>I should probably run diagnostic mode on the <BR>computer to see if I get any codes.<P>Mark S.||||reg||
Z||000001||mwbackus||09-07-1999||08:10 PM||||I would say the your cat converter has seen its day.  I would also suggest an oil change and maybe even a minor tune up (cap, rotor & plugs).  BTW, Summit Racing has Catco Hi-flo converters for $49.99 (CTO-6004 for 88 V6), should cost less than $50 for install at a local shop.  This would result in a total cost of replacement that is less than the average price of a converter alone.<P>Mike<BR>88 Formula (108k)||||reg||
Z||000002||AL||09-08-1999||06:18 AM||||I SUGGEST A MAJOR TUNE UP,PLUS GET THE CARS INJECTORS CLEAN,THAT WAY IF THERE IS BUILD UP IN YOUR INTAKE IT WILL CLEAN IT.EMISSIONS COME FROM A POORLY TUNED ENGINE,IF THE CAR IS TUNED UP RIGHT, IT WILL CUT THE EMMISIONS.THE CAT IS TO BURN VAPOR THAT IS LEFT OVER FROM THE ENGINE IT IS NOT A FILTER FOR A POOR RUNNING ENGINE||||reg||
Z||000003||2FAST4U||09-08-1999||07:16 AM||||Since you failed the HC ( unburned hydrocarbons) part of the test I'D do a leakdown / compression test to make sure you don't have worn rings oo excessivly leaking valve stems on a car with this many miles on it. Just because it does'nt smoke, does'nt mean you don't have excessive blow by.<p>[This message has been edited by 2FAST4U (edited 09-08-1999).]||||reg||
Z||000004||mwbackus||09-08-1999||10:38 AM||||If you take time to look at the emissions test results that Mark had the forsight to provide, you will see that the hydrocarbon levels reduce dramatically with higher rpm.<BR>In addition, the carbon monoxide levels drop to very near passing with the increased rpm/load.  <BR>     This is very typical of a catalyst failure, and these levels are simply not high enough to indicate any type of severe mechanical problem.  A new cat is actually a VERY good filter for the emissions of a poor running engine, thats what they do, although "filter" would be an incorrect description.  Actually, a chemical reaction takes place on the material bed inside the cat.  For example, Carbon Monoxide is oxidized as it passes through the catalyst bed and becomes CO2, a harmless gas.  If none of components are "extremely high" in relative comparison to the others, a tune up, oil change, and a new cat converter is the best approach for attacking the problem.<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||
Z||000005||mshill||09-08-1999||05:54 PM||||Good discussion.  Each time I take on a new project like this, I learn more and more.  I guess that is one of the benefits of doing some of your own work.<P>UPDATE:  I was looking into the engine compartment while the car was idling a couple of nights ago, and saw a significant amount of arcing from the base of each plug out to the head.  Since I was going to replace most everything on the engine anyway, I went and got an Accel Performance Coil and cap/rotor, Nascar Wires and AC Delco Rapid Fire plugs.  In replacing the plugs/wires/cap/rotor I found tremendous amounts of corrosion on the cap contacts (I bet I lost 50% of the spark energy just at the cap terminals).  I am sure this will help.<P>ADDITIONALLY, I inspected the (dreaded) EGR tube and it looks like it is cracked at the base of the intake manifold flange.  I can see a gap that looks like it extends about 25% of the radius of the tube just below the flange.   <BR>Would a leak in the EGR tube cause a lack of vacuum thus not actuating the EGR valve?  <BR>Would I be able to hear an exhaust leak through there if the EGR valve was actuated?<BR>Could this have also contributed to my test failure?<P><BR>Thanks again.<P>||||reg||
Z||000006||theogre||09-08-1999||05:38 PM||||That crack would cause a vac leak period, and that alone will screw up the whole system.||||reg||
Z||000007||mshill||09-09-1999||01:22 AM||||Wouldn't I get a check engine light if the EGR vacuum was messed up?<P>I have read a couple of threads on this topic, is there an easy way around this? What about that silver epoxy stuff used on cracked  blocks with coolant leaks?<P>HELP!||||reg||
Z||000008||mwbackus||09-09-1999||01:59 AM||||When I first got my formula, I had a racing clutch put in.  I did not have the facilities to do it myself at the time.  On the drive home, I noticed a rather high idle, several miles later I had a severe overheat. <BR>     I finally got it home, and checked everything out throughly.  I found the EGR tube had been cracked completly off where it attaches to the intake plenum.  I took it back the next day and after I recited several 4 letter words they agreed to replace it free.  No codes were ever set during the time I drove it with the broken tube.  <BR>     I believe that a code will only set if there is a fault in the EGR vacuum selenoid or vacuum lines used to engage the EGR valve.  A cracked EGR tube would be equivlent to any major vacuum leak, and the ECM would just attempt to compensate by adjusting the air/fuel ratio accordingly. <P>Mike ||||reg||
Z||000009||mshill||10-12-1999||07:03 PM||||Failed CA emissions again even after complete tuneup, new cap/rotor, wires, plugs, rebuilt distributor and EGR tube fixed.  I also decided to do a complete compression test and this engine with 188K miles is incredible ( 138-148 psi) across all six cylinders.<P>I figure that it has to be the catalytic converter.  I am looking for a free-flow (or low flow) cat to have installed.<P>||||reg||8
Z||000010||TOM||10-12-1999||07:28 PM||||You mean high flow catalytic convertor||||reg||1
Z||000011||mwbackus||10-12-1999||07:41 PM||||Summit has the Catco high flow converters for $49.99.  IMO, they are every bit as good as the Dynomax, at less than half the cost.<BR>Again, it was obvious to me that a new cat would have corrected your emission failure problems...oh well.<P>Mike<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 10-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Patrick||10-12-1999||08:09 PM||||<BR>This is from a post of mine here months ago:<P>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;<P>... I tried and tried to get the Fiero to pass (the emission test) with all the tricks I had learned over the years, but I finally had to admit defeat and I took the car to a Pontiac dealership for their mechanics to deal with. Well, $400 later I returned to the testing station where the Fiero not only scored worse on the emission tests, but the damn thing wouldn't even idle properly. Because I had spent the money at a "Certified" repair center, I got a conditional "pass" for the year, but what a friggen joke. I had to undo what the damn dealership mechanics did to make the car run better. A year later I went to an independent shop to try my luck. They determined that the "cat" needed replacement. I replaced it and the car actually passed the smog test legitimately. This is probably what it needed the year before...<P>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;<P>Mark, sorry I didn't repost this earlier, but you didn't listen to Mike anyway... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000013||Lynx||10-12-1999||08:33 PM||||Im realy startin to love where i live. Just a state above you guys and NO TESTS. Never have to worry about that bull crap.||||reg||6
Z||000014||Patrick||10-12-1999||08:41 PM||||<BR>Well Lynx, you're actually <B>below</B> where I live. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Regarding the emission tests, yes they're a damn nuisance, but I'm hoping that because of them that we can help keep the air more breathable. Especially in the large metropolitan areas.||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||10-13-1999||12:36 AM||||Assuming the O2 is working, I'd replace the cat. A new cat does wonders.<P>As far as emission testing (I'm in CA), I have no problem with it at all. If you understand how the it works, it's a no brainer. It's only closed loop testing anyway, even on the treadmill.<P>Mr. Green Jeans.||||reg||1
Z||000016||fieroshop||10-13-1999||02:13 AM||||Well as far as I can see you need a cat, preferably a free flow cat. I repair about 3 cars a month for the reason of smog testing and in 99% of the cases it has been the cat||||reg||6
Z||000017||mshill||10-13-1999||02:48 PM||||I don't feel too bad having replaced the other items (cap/rotor, wires, plugs, distributor and EGR tube), they all needed it anyway.  I just wished I had coughed up the $100 to replace the cat instead of wasting my free retest.<P>I got a cat this morning and will be installing this weekend.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Cooter||10-13-1999||03:23 PM||||I live in one of the states without emission testing yet. It is a mixed blessing. Yes, not having to battle the standards is great, but there is a bunch of junk on the roads here that is unsafe. Every accident that I have ever been in has involved someone driving an uninsured, unsafe vehicle hitting me. I am for safety inspections, but the Environmental Terriorism is a bit too much at times.||||reg||10
Z||000019||Patrick||10-13-1999||07:49 PM||||<BR>Cooter, here in the Vancouver BC area, we used to have "safety" inspections every year for our cars. Due to government cutbacks, that came to an end years ago. The emission tests we have now are <B>just</B> that, <B>emission</B> tests. We now have clean running cars with no brakes and wheels about to fall off! Go figure, eh?||||reg||4
